Title: GRAILS
Author: Operator Mullen
Draft date: 2025-07-09
Contact: coywolffuturist

---

FADE IN:

EXT. SUBURBAN GARAGE SALE - EARLY MORNING

A Reseda front lawn, 7 AM. Card tables weighted with other people's pasts. Mismatched china. A box of eight-track tapes. A hand-lettered sign: EVERYTHING MUST GO.

A SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD in a sleep-wrinkled hoodie moves through the tables without urgency. He's not looking for anything. That's when you find things.

He stops.

On a folding table, between a broken humidifier and a stack of Reader's Digests: a wooden cigar box, latched shut. Old. Scuffed along every corner.

He opens it.

Inside: baseball cards. Hundreds. A rubber band holds the stacks. He fans through them with a practiced thumb -- the reflex of someone who has done this a thousand times and found nothing.

His thumb stops.

CLOSE ON: a card. The image worn at the edges but the face clear -- young, nineteen, pre-everything. 1989 Upper Deck. Ken Griffey Jr. Rookie.

The kid's face goes blank. The blankness of someone holding a fact too large to process.

SELLER (O.S.)
Quarter a card. Dollar for the whole box.

SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD
(without looking up)
I'll take the box.

He sets a dollar on the table. His hand is steady.

CUT TO:

INT. KID'S CAR - CONTINUOUS

He's parked half a block away. Engine off. Phone out. He's filming the card -- shaky, too close, too excited -- and posting before he's thought it through.

ON SCREEN: the TikTok upload bar filling. Caption: "bro found at a garage sale... is this real??"

He hits post.

The notifications begin.

CUT TO:

INT. KEVIN'S APARTMENT - LOS ANGELES - DAY

KEVIN (40s, the particular handsomeness of a man who has never quite committed to anything) is eating cereal standing over his kitchen sink, scrolling.

He stops scrolling.

He watches the video twice. Then a third time.

He sets the bowl down. He's already typing.

CUT TO:

EXT. VALLEY PARKING LOT - DAY

A strip mall behind a Panda Express. Kevin stands next to his car. The kid pulls up.

Kevin doesn't rush. He examines the card through a loupe -- slow, methodical -- while the kid shifts his weight from foot to foot.

KEVIN
(not looking up)
Where'd you get it?

SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD
Garage sale. Reseda. Lady was asking a dollar for the box.

KEVIN
You pay that?

SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD
Yeah.

KEVIN
(a beat, still looking)
You got lucky.

He snaps the loupe shut. Pulls an envelope from his jacket.

KEVIN (CONT'D)
It's damaged. Top edge. Authenticity's a question until someone grades it.

SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD
How much?

Kevin hands him the envelope without ceremony. The kid opens it.

SEVENTEEN-YEAR-OLD (CONT'D)
(exhale)
Yeah. Yeah, okay.

Kevin is already walking back to his car, the card now in a protective sleeve in his breast pocket. He's on his phone.

KEVIN
(into phone)
I need the best card restorer in LA. 

(beat)

No -- not appraiser. Restorer. Someone who knows what they're doing.

CUT TO:

INT. KEVIN'S CAR - MOVING - DAY

Kevin drives. A list on his phone's cracked screen. He crosses names off one by one. Makes calls. Gets disconnected. Calls back.

He circles one name.

CUT TO:

EXT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE - DAY

A strip mall in Burbank. The kind that was thriving in 1994 and has been slowly losing wars ever since. On the left: a RadioShack sign hangs by a single wire over a gutted storefront. On the right: a Spirit Halloween banner peels off what used to be a department store.

In the middle: ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE. The windows are clean. Inside, warm amber light.

Kevin pulls in.

CUT TO:

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

The smell hits you first: acid-free plastic, aged cardboard, the sweet chalky ghost of forty-year-old bubblegum dust.

Display cases running the length of the shop. Framed jerseys. Slabbed cards in museum lighting. A small fan oscillating in the corner, moving nothing.

Behind the counter: ZACH (late 30s, sun-faded 1984 Durham Bulls tee, the focused calm of someone who chose this life deliberately). He's deep in conversation with a THREE-GENERATION FAMILY -- a GRANDFATHER in a windbreaker, a FATHER with tired eyes, a TEN-YEAR-OLD SON who is watching everything.

Between them: shoeboxes. The grandfather's hoard.

Kevin approaches. Sets the box on the counter. Zach glances up once -- acknowledging, not stopping.

ZACH
Give me five minutes.

Kevin nods. He finds a chair at the back of the shop.

ZACH (CONT'D)
(returning to the family)
Okay. The highlights.

He reaches into the nearest shoebox. Extracts a card with two fingers.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Bill Ripken. 1989 Fleer. The error card -- "Fuck Face" on the bat knob. They tried to black it out in later prints.

The grandfather grins. The ten-year-old mouths the words silently.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Randy Johnson. 1989 Fleer. The Marlboro ad on the outfield wall. They airbrushed it in Canadian prints.

FATHER
(leaning forward)
So those are valuable?

Zach sets them down carefully.

ZACH
They're 5.5s. Maybe 6 on a good day.

(beat)

Total value for the lot?

He surveys the boxes.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Four, five hundred dollars.

The hope leaves the father's face. He looks at his son. The ten-year-old doesn't understand the numbers but he understands his father's face.

GRANDFATHER
(quietly)
I thought...

ZACH
I know.

(beat)

Wait.

He reaches under the counter. Brings up a sealed slab -- the kind with the holographic label and the tamper-evident case. Inside it: a card. Crisp. Centered. Glowing in the amber light.

1989 Upper Deck. Ken Griffey Jr. Rookie. 

The father's breath catches.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Your dad brought this to me six months ago. Asked if I could see what I could do.

He sets it on the counter between them.

ZACH (CONT'D)
I cleaned the surface. Pressed the fibers. In this slab, it grades a 9.

He turns the slab over. On the back: a small holographic log.

ZACH (CONT'D)
But I logged it. "Restored." It's in the database. I won't pass off restoration as factory-original. It won't fetch the $10,000 a Natural 10 would.

(beat)

But it's yours. And it's perfect.

The father reaches across the counter. He takes the slab in both hands.

A long moment.

The grandfather steps toward him. The father doesn't move. The grandfather puts his arms around him from behind, and the father's shoulders drop, and neither of them say anything.

The ten-year-old watches. He's trying to understand. He's never seen his father cry over cardboard.

Then something shifts in his face -- the way it does when a door opens in a wall you thought was solid -- and he understands that this was never about cardboard.

Kevin, in the chair at the back, checks his phone.

He shifts in his seat.

Checks again.

The most important transaction of his life is being held up by an old man crying.

SMASH CUT TO:

TITLE CARD: GRAILS

FADE OUT.

